1
00:00:01,960 --> 00:00:04,671
<i>I'm an early bird and I'm a night owl.</i>

2
00:00:04,755 --> 00:00:07,508
<i>So I'm wise and I have worms.</i>

3
00:00:09,718 --> 00:00:11,136
Oh, breakfast.

4
00:00:11,220 --> 00:00:13,263
I got your sausage, egg and cheese biscuit.

5
00:00:13,347 --> 00:00:16,308
Yummy, yummy, thank you, Ryan.

6
00:00:17,684 --> 00:00:21,188
What was the thing you needed me to come in early for?

7
00:00:21,772 --> 00:00:22,773
Um...

8
00:00:23,941 --> 00:00:27,236
The sausage, egg and cheese biscuit. But, thank you.

9
00:00:27,319 --> 00:00:32,241
And why don't you just take a couple hours. Office is yours.

10
00:00:32,699 --> 00:00:37,287
<i>Home Alone, Risky Business.</i> Take your pants off, run around.

11
00:00:38,705 --> 00:00:40,582
Whatever you want to do.

12
00:00:40,666 --> 00:00:42,866
I'm just going to take a nap in my car till work starts.

13
00:00:42,918 --> 00:00:44,044
Okay.

14
00:00:45,921 --> 00:00:49,466
See? Healthier, got to watch those carbs.

15
00:01:23,709 --> 00:01:28,297
Today, I, Michael Scott, am becoming a homeowner, investing in real estate.

16
00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:29,798
Diversifying. Smart.

17
00:01:29,881 --> 00:01:31,967
Yes, it is. Yes, it is.

18
00:01:32,801 --> 00:01:35,429
It is very important to own property.

19
00:01:35,721 --> 00:01:39,308
Back in olden days, they would not even let you vote

20
00:01:39,391 --> 00:01:42,936
unless you owned property, and they'd throw you in the stocks and humiliate you.

21
00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:44,146
And it worked. Yes.

22
00:01:44,229 --> 00:01:46,416
They should bring the stocks back, people would obey the law,

23
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:47,500
there'd be less troublemakers.

24
00:01:47,524 --> 00:01:49,067
Maybe.

25
00:01:56,074 --> 00:01:59,036
<i>Every so often, Jim dies of boredom.</i>

26
00:01:59,828 --> 00:02:03,248
I think today it was the expense reports that did him in.

27
00:02:03,332 --> 00:02:06,501
And our deal is, that it's up to me to revive him.

28
00:02:07,377 --> 00:02:09,463
You see Dwight's coffee mug?

29
00:02:09,546 --> 00:02:12,049
Sometimes when he's not here, I try to throw stuff in it.

30
00:02:12,132 --> 00:02:13,342
No way.

31
00:02:15,302 --> 00:02:16,428
Let's do this.

32
00:02:22,184 --> 00:02:24,436
Here, try paperclips.

33
00:02:24,895 --> 00:02:27,773
Oh, wait, this message, for Dwight.

34
00:02:27,856 --> 00:02:28,857
Perfect.

35
00:02:32,361 --> 00:02:33,362
You should go.

36
00:02:33,445 --> 00:02:35,113
Yes, yes. Final walk-through.

37
00:02:35,822 --> 00:02:36,982
Sign the papers at the condo.

38
00:02:37,032 --> 00:02:38,283
You have your lawyer there?

39
00:02:38,367 --> 00:02:39,368
I don't need one.

40
00:02:39,451 --> 00:02:40,511
Can I be your representative?

41
00:02:40,535 --> 00:02:42,138
I think I should be there. I don't need representation.

42
00:02:42,162 --> 00:02:43,514
No, no. No, I'm good. I can make sure

43
00:02:43,538 --> 00:02:45,178
things are up to code. No, Dwight. Please.

44
00:02:45,248 --> 00:02:47,000
I'm always the guy that you rely on at work.

45
00:02:47,084 --> 00:02:49,795
Well, this isn't about work. This is closing on a condo.

46
00:02:49,878 --> 00:02:51,213
It's completely personal.

47
00:02:51,296 --> 00:02:53,465
So, you're taking a personal day?

48
00:02:54,216 --> 00:02:56,718
Except that it's about my living arrangement

49
00:02:56,802 --> 00:03:01,390
and as boss I need to have a living arrangement in order to do work.

50
00:03:01,473 --> 00:03:03,642
Please. I'll make you proud.

51
00:03:04,726 --> 00:03:06,019
Okay, fine. Yes, you can come.

52
00:03:06,103 --> 00:03:07,229
Yes. As your representative?

53
00:03:07,312 --> 00:03:08,814
As my associate. Same thing.

54
00:03:08,897 --> 00:03:10,190
No, it is not.

55
00:03:10,273 --> 00:03:13,568
I have been Michael's number two guy for about five years

56
00:03:13,652 --> 00:03:15,320
and we make a great team.

57
00:03:15,404 --> 00:03:17,698
Why, we're like one of those classic, famous teams.

58
00:03:17,823 --> 00:03:21,410
He's like Mozart and I'm like Mozart's friend.

59
00:03:22,577 --> 00:03:25,497
No, I'm like Butch Cassidy,

60
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:28,333
and Michael is like Mozart.

61
00:03:29,292 --> 00:03:33,130
You try and hurt Mozart, you're gonna get a bullet in your head,

62
00:03:33,213 --> 00:03:35,257
courtesy of Butch Cassidy.

63
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:38,760
Most honorable Pamela.

64
00:03:39,302 --> 00:03:41,680
Not offensive, because that's the way they talk in movies.

65
00:03:41,763 --> 00:03:43,265
You headed out? We are.

66
00:03:43,348 --> 00:03:45,600
Dwight and I are going to the big thing.

67
00:03:45,726 --> 00:03:48,687
So, why don't you have everybody work on their expense reports

68
00:03:48,770 --> 00:03:50,814
and I'd like them in by the end of the day. Okay.

69
00:03:50,897 --> 00:03:52,017
Very good. Have a great time.

70
00:03:52,065 --> 00:03:55,026
We will. Did you do the thing I asked you to do about the magazines?

71
00:03:55,110 --> 00:03:57,112
Yeah, I changed them to your new address. Good.

72
00:03:57,195 --> 00:03:59,656
<i>The Small Business Man?</i> Yep.

73
00:04:00,407 --> 00:04:01,992
<i>Maxim?</i>

74
00:04:02,075 --> 00:04:03,785
<i>American Way? CRACKED?</i>

75
00:04:04,411 --> 00:04:07,748
Yes, I changed your <i>CRACKED</i> magazine subscription.

76
00:04:08,874 --> 00:04:11,543
How about <i>Fine Arts</i>

77
00:04:13,086 --> 00:04:15,130
<i>Aficionado Monthly?</i>

78
00:04:16,923 --> 00:04:20,594
No? Okay. Well, could you get on that, 'cause I don't just read <i>CRACKED.</i>

79
00:04:20,677 --> 00:04:21,720
Thank you. Yeah.

80
00:04:21,803 --> 00:04:23,597
Okay. See you soon.

81
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:25,891
What kind of shocks you got on this baby?

82
00:04:25,974 --> 00:04:27,934
I don't know, regular, normal ones.

83
00:04:28,643 --> 00:04:31,438
Nothing fancy, not my style.

84
00:04:32,522 --> 00:04:34,362
What are you doing? I want to put the top down.

85
00:04:34,441 --> 00:04:37,611
What are you... No, Dwight. It's 50 degrees outside, please.

86
00:04:37,694 --> 00:04:39,112
But then no one can see us.

87
00:04:39,196 --> 00:04:41,531
I... Just... Would you put it up?

88
00:04:42,365 --> 00:04:45,702
Okay, fine, just leave it down. Whiner.

89
00:04:47,954 --> 00:04:50,499
Check it out. <i>Terminator.</i>

90
00:04:50,874 --> 00:04:54,044
I do not understand what you spend your money on.

91
00:04:55,670 --> 00:04:59,841
Hey, Oscar. These new expense reports, do we really have to go back to last quarter?

92
00:04:59,925 --> 00:05:02,636
Yeah, it's a terrible system, I know.

93
00:05:04,304 --> 00:05:06,765
What does "2005 Season" mean?

94
00:05:07,974 --> 00:05:09,076
Wait a minute. What is this?

95
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:10,310
It's a scoreboard.

96
00:05:10,393 --> 00:05:11,394
What?

97
00:05:11,478 --> 00:05:13,271
Kevin and I play this paper football game

98
00:05:13,355 --> 00:05:14,564
when Michael's out. Really?

99
00:05:14,648 --> 00:05:17,359
Or when we're bored. Oh, my God.

100
00:05:17,651 --> 00:05:19,736
Wait, this goes back two years.

101
00:05:19,903 --> 00:05:20,904
We're bored a lot.

102
00:05:23,532 --> 00:05:24,533
Sweet.

103
00:05:24,616 --> 00:05:26,368
Yes, so close.

104
00:05:27,202 --> 00:05:31,081
I really love the "paper triangle flicking and hitting things" game.

105
00:05:31,164 --> 00:05:32,749
Yeah. We call it "Hate Ball."

106
00:05:32,833 --> 00:05:33,875
Why?

107
00:05:33,959 --> 00:05:36,503
Because of how much Angela hates it.

108
00:05:37,170 --> 00:05:39,047
Hey, do you guys have any other games?

109
00:05:39,130 --> 00:05:42,801
Sometimes we play "who can put the most M&M's in their mouth."

110
00:05:42,884 --> 00:05:44,052
You play that.

111
00:05:44,135 --> 00:05:47,347
You should ask Toby to teach you "Dunder Ball."

112
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:50,308
Home, sweet home.

113
00:05:51,434 --> 00:05:52,561
Which one's yours?

114
00:05:53,270 --> 00:05:57,566
Right there. My sanctuary, my party-pad.

115
00:05:59,067 --> 00:06:03,989
Someday, I can just see my grandkids learning how to walk out here.

116
00:06:05,031 --> 00:06:09,119
Hang a swing from this tree, push them back...

117
00:06:11,037 --> 00:06:13,999
Wait. No, it's this one right here.

118
00:06:14,916 --> 00:06:16,626
Home, sweet home.

119
00:06:20,589 --> 00:06:23,466
So that's what this sound is all day.

120
00:06:23,633 --> 00:06:26,219
Michael, this is Bill. He's the head of the condo association.

121
00:06:26,303 --> 00:06:29,931
Oh, hey, how are you? Nice to meet you, Bill. Bill, Mr. Bill.

122
00:06:30,015 --> 00:06:33,226
Oh, no, Mr. Bill!

123
00:06:35,437 --> 00:06:38,189
<i>SNL.</i> When they pull him apart?

124
00:06:39,649 --> 00:06:41,902
He would always get rolled over by something.

125
00:06:42,402 --> 00:06:44,112
Nice to meet you. Nice to meet you, too.

126
00:06:44,195 --> 00:06:45,906
This is smaller than your old place.

127
00:06:45,989 --> 00:06:48,074
Yes, well, I'm buying it. I'm not renting it.

128
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:50,702
So, it's still an upgrade.

129
00:06:51,828 --> 00:06:55,498
He doesn't know anything about property ownership. He's kind of an idiot.

130
00:06:56,166 --> 00:06:57,834
<i>Actually, I do own property.</i>

131
00:06:58,418 --> 00:07:01,421
My grandfather left me a 60 acre working beet farm.

132
00:07:01,504 --> 00:07:03,506
<i>I run it with my cousin Moes.</i>

133
00:07:03,590 --> 00:07:06,801
We sell beets to local stores and restaurants.

134
00:07:07,886 --> 00:07:09,888
It's a nice little farm.

135
00:07:10,889 --> 00:07:13,433
Sometimes teenagers use it for sex.

136
00:07:13,767 --> 00:07:15,101
Are we ready to sign some papers?

137
00:07:15,185 --> 00:07:17,938
Actually, no, we have a couple of questions.

138
00:07:18,063 --> 00:07:19,606
About the neighborhood.

139
00:07:19,689 --> 00:07:20,690
It's very safe.

140
00:07:20,774 --> 00:07:22,400
It's very clean.

141
00:07:22,484 --> 00:07:25,737
Also, it's very accepting of all lifestyles.

142
00:07:27,822 --> 00:07:30,450
It's a very gay-friendly neighborhood.

143
00:07:31,368 --> 00:07:33,203
Oh, good, that's good.

144
00:07:34,245 --> 00:07:36,748
It's good to be accommodating of that.

145
00:07:37,165 --> 00:07:39,834
Let's go check out the master bedroom.

146
00:07:40,210 --> 00:07:43,838
Stanley, I just played Dunder Ball with Toby.

147
00:07:43,922 --> 00:07:45,107
What about you? You got any games?

148
00:07:45,131 --> 00:07:46,174
Yeah, I got a game.

149
00:07:46,257 --> 00:07:49,386
It's called "work hard, so my kids can go to college."

150
00:07:50,053 --> 00:07:51,346
Fair enough.

151
00:07:52,514 --> 00:07:56,476
This, my friends, is the master bedroom.

152
00:07:56,559 --> 00:08:00,563
Check out the cathedral ceilings. Those are, like, 17 feet high.

153
00:08:00,647 --> 00:08:05,151
We have cable readiness right there. I'm going to totally pimp this place out.

154
00:08:05,235 --> 00:08:07,654
I'm going to put a surround sound system.

155
00:08:07,779 --> 00:08:10,323
I'm going to put a plasma screen right against this wall.

156
00:08:10,407 --> 00:08:12,426
Oh, terrible idea. Here's where I'm putting my bed, right here.

157
00:08:12,450 --> 00:08:14,619
No, no, no, no, this is a shared wall.

158
00:08:14,703 --> 00:08:16,329
Neighbor throws his wife into the wall,

159
00:08:16,413 --> 00:08:18,373
plasma screen hits the floor, totally smashed.

160
00:08:18,456 --> 00:08:19,791
Well, then I will get a warranty.

161
00:08:19,874 --> 00:08:20,917
Warranties don't cover it.

162
00:08:21,001 --> 00:08:22,002
Plus they're a rip-off.

163
00:08:22,085 --> 00:08:23,187
Well, then I won't get a warranty.

164
00:08:23,211 --> 00:08:24,689
Wait. So, that's the problem, it's solved. What?

165
00:08:24,713 --> 00:08:25,773
I don't hear you practicing.

166
00:08:25,797 --> 00:08:28,091
Listen. Can you hear that?

167
00:08:29,592 --> 00:08:30,802
Oh, man,

168
00:08:31,928 --> 00:08:33,972
these babies are thin.

169
00:08:45,859 --> 00:08:49,571
This scented candle, which I found in the men's bathroom,

170
00:08:51,322 --> 00:08:54,492
represents the eternal

171
00:08:55,368 --> 00:08:58,121
burning of competition,

172
00:08:58,455 --> 00:08:59,581
or something.

173
00:08:59,664 --> 00:09:00,665
It smells like cookies.

174
00:09:01,166 --> 00:09:04,544
Yes, it does. Yes, it does, my friend.

175
00:09:05,211 --> 00:09:09,883
Okay, we will be competing for gold, silver and bronze yogurt lids.

176
00:09:10,341 --> 00:09:12,385
Now, the bronze are really blue

177
00:09:12,469 --> 00:09:14,888
and they're also the backside of the gold, so no flipping.

178
00:09:14,971 --> 00:09:16,723
Okay, honor system.

179
00:09:21,519 --> 00:09:22,771
<i>I do play games.</i>

180
00:09:23,063 --> 00:09:26,399
I sing and I dangle things in front of my cats.

181
00:09:26,733 --> 00:09:29,527
I play lots of games, just not at work.

182
00:09:30,070 --> 00:09:31,946
Let the games begin.

183
00:09:37,994 --> 00:09:40,246
And then I just need you to sign here at this arrow.

184
00:09:40,330 --> 00:09:42,123
What kind of mortgage did you get?

185
00:09:42,582 --> 00:09:43,583
Ten year.

186
00:09:44,334 --> 00:09:47,504
Well, 10 over 30. So 30 year total.

187
00:09:47,712 --> 00:09:48,713
What? What?

188
00:09:49,297 --> 00:09:51,132
You said 10.

189
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:56,304
Ten year fixed, over 30, 30 year total.

190
00:09:56,387 --> 00:09:57,722
Oh, 30 years.

191
00:09:57,806 --> 00:09:59,265
Okay, okay, okay.

192
00:09:59,599 --> 00:10:02,352
Wow, you'll be paying this off in your mid-70s.

193
00:10:02,435 --> 00:10:04,062
All right.

194
00:10:04,145 --> 00:10:06,272
Forget about retiring when you're 65.

195
00:10:06,439 --> 00:10:08,359
Hey, I have an idea. You know that extra bedroom?

196
00:10:08,399 --> 00:10:11,611
If the whole girlfriend thing never happens, that's where the nurse can live.

197
00:10:11,694 --> 00:10:14,781
Okay, all right. Oh, boy.

198
00:10:14,906 --> 00:10:15,907
Okay, now, this is it.

199
00:10:15,990 --> 00:10:16,991
Whenever you're ready.

200
00:10:17,075 --> 00:10:18,201
Yeah.

201
00:10:19,077 --> 00:10:22,497
Oh, okay, is that supposed to come off?

202
00:10:22,622 --> 00:10:23,623
Actually, yeah.

203
00:10:23,706 --> 00:10:24,986
Hey, look, cool, carpenter ants.

204
00:10:25,750 --> 00:10:29,212
I'm going to take a little breather for a second, excuse me.

205
00:10:29,337 --> 00:10:30,922
We'll be here waiting for you.

206
00:10:31,005 --> 00:10:32,006
Oh, man.

207
00:10:32,465 --> 00:10:34,300
A 30-year mortgage at Michael's age

208
00:10:34,384 --> 00:10:36,553
essentially means that he's buying a coffin.

209
00:10:36,803 --> 00:10:40,557
Now, if I were buying my coffin, I would get one with thicker walls

210
00:10:40,807 --> 00:10:43,143
so you couldn't hear the other dead people.

211
00:10:46,855 --> 00:10:48,606
Whenever you're ready, Michael.

212
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:54,863
You have what is the national sport of Icelandic paper companies.

213
00:10:55,822 --> 00:10:58,908
And I'm blanking on the name. Could you help me out, Pam?

214
00:10:58,992 --> 00:11:02,954
Jim, they refer to it as "Flonkerton."

215
00:11:03,705 --> 00:11:06,416
In English, "box-of-paper snowshoe racing."

216
00:11:07,041 --> 00:11:09,669
Fair enough, but I like Flonkerton.

217
00:11:10,044 --> 00:11:11,963
The thing about Jim is

218
00:11:12,839 --> 00:11:16,009
when he's excited about something, like the Office Olympics,

219
00:11:16,092 --> 00:11:19,721
<i>he gets really into it and he does a really great job.</i>

220
00:11:20,305 --> 00:11:23,141
But the problem with Jim is that he works here,

221
00:11:23,224 --> 00:11:25,393
so that hardly ever happens.

222
00:11:25,977 --> 00:11:29,731
So, who will be challenging Kevin in Flonkerton?

223
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:31,941
Anyone? I'll do it.

224
00:11:32,025 --> 00:11:33,359
Yes, Phyllis.

225
00:11:33,443 --> 00:11:34,736
Phyllis.

226
00:11:36,821 --> 00:11:41,075
Phyllis, if you just put your foot right through here, right through the flonk.

227
00:11:41,242 --> 00:11:44,287
The ceilings are lower than they were last week.

228
00:11:44,370 --> 00:11:45,413
That... I don't...

229
00:11:45,496 --> 00:11:46,765
What? I don't know if you showed me

230
00:11:46,789 --> 00:11:48,082
the same unit or not.

231
00:11:48,166 --> 00:11:49,876
Michael, this is the unit you saw.

232
00:11:49,959 --> 00:11:52,754
And where are all the hot people?

233
00:11:52,837 --> 00:11:55,965
I was told that there would be all these attractive singles.

234
00:11:56,174 --> 00:11:57,610
Who told you that? And as far as I can tell,

235
00:11:57,634 --> 00:11:59,552
I'm the best-looking person here.

236
00:11:59,636 --> 00:12:01,429
There's a basic principle in real estate,

237
00:12:01,512 --> 00:12:04,766
that you should never be the best-looking person in the development.

238
00:12:04,933 --> 00:12:06,184
It's just sort of common sense.

239
00:12:06,267 --> 00:12:10,939
Because if you are, then you've no place to go but down.

240
00:12:11,731 --> 00:12:12,899
Is this a financial thing?

241
00:12:12,982 --> 00:12:16,486
If it's a financial thing, what some people do is they rent out the third bedroom.

242
00:12:16,569 --> 00:12:17,796
That's an extra income for you. No, no.

243
00:12:17,820 --> 00:12:20,531
I'm not going to rent the third bedroom.

244
00:12:20,615 --> 00:12:24,035
I want a price reduction or I am a-walking.

245
00:12:24,661 --> 00:12:28,498
You will lose $7,000 if you walk away right now.

246
00:12:32,335 --> 00:12:35,505
I made the right decision. I'm glad I signed.

247
00:12:35,755 --> 00:12:38,174
Look, I'm a homeowner. Right?

248
00:12:38,383 --> 00:12:41,386
Good to be a homeowner, diversified. This is good.

249
00:12:41,803 --> 00:12:43,596
This is fun. We're having fun.

250
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:44,681
Totally having fun.

251
00:12:45,223 --> 00:12:47,767
Can you imagine those poor saps stuck at the office today?

252
00:12:49,978 --> 00:12:51,271
Here we go. Here we go.

253
00:12:51,354 --> 00:12:52,522
Go, go, go!

254
00:12:52,605 --> 00:12:55,692
Pair of shoes. Dig deep. Dig deep.

255
00:12:58,319 --> 00:13:00,405
It's Phyllis, Phyllis by a nose.

256
00:13:00,530 --> 00:13:03,533
Gold medal in Flernenton. Flonkerton.

257
00:13:03,616 --> 00:13:05,493
Thank you, delegate from Iceland.

258
00:13:13,543 --> 00:13:16,671
Wow, okay, no one else should even try.

259
00:13:16,754 --> 00:13:19,924
Gold medals. Give him medals.

260
00:13:20,925 --> 00:13:21,926
Wow.

261
00:13:24,345 --> 00:13:27,682
There's something else, Dwight, I want to talk to you about.

262
00:13:27,765 --> 00:13:30,643
I have a surprise for you, for helping me out today.

263
00:13:30,727 --> 00:13:31,811
Oh, you didn't have to...

264
00:13:31,894 --> 00:13:33,604
No, no, I insist, I insist.

265
00:13:33,688 --> 00:13:35,982
Because you've really done some great work, great work.

266
00:13:36,065 --> 00:13:38,693
And that is why I'm going to let you

267
00:13:38,776 --> 00:13:42,363
move into my third bedroom and pay me rent.

268
00:13:44,657 --> 00:13:45,867
Why did I do it?

269
00:13:45,950 --> 00:13:49,329
Because I believe in rewarding people for their efforts.

270
00:13:50,121 --> 00:13:53,833
I rewarded Dwight with the room

271
00:13:54,375 --> 00:13:56,919
and he is rewarding me back

272
00:13:57,211 --> 00:13:59,797
with $500 plus utilities.

273
00:14:00,340 --> 00:14:01,966
I don't even know what to say.

274
00:14:02,050 --> 00:14:05,595
I'm thinking, lock you into a four-year commitment.

275
00:14:05,678 --> 00:14:07,513
We'll go month-to-month after that.

276
00:14:07,597 --> 00:14:11,434
Or until I start dating, have a girlfriend and then, you're...

277
00:14:11,768 --> 00:14:13,394
You know, you're gone. So...

278
00:14:13,478 --> 00:14:16,022
Question. Where can I put my terrarium?

279
00:14:18,274 --> 00:14:20,068
What the hell is a terrarium?

280
00:14:20,193 --> 00:14:22,070
It's a fish tank for snakes and lizards.

281
00:14:22,779 --> 00:14:24,530
Oh, so, an aquarium?

282
00:14:26,407 --> 00:14:30,328
That will not come into this place, okay?

283
00:14:30,787 --> 00:14:32,080
Question. Oh, my God.

284
00:14:32,163 --> 00:14:36,209
My grandparents left me a large number of armoires.

285
00:14:36,292 --> 00:14:37,686
Are you sure you don't want to play?

286
00:14:37,710 --> 00:14:38,711
I'm sure.

287
00:14:38,836 --> 00:14:41,547
Come on, Angela, don't you have a game?

288
00:14:41,631 --> 00:14:43,466
I have one, yes.

289
00:14:43,549 --> 00:14:44,884
Well, let's play. What is it?

290
00:14:45,426 --> 00:14:46,928
I call it "Pam-Pong."

291
00:14:47,011 --> 00:14:49,013
I count how many times Jim gets up from his desk

292
00:14:49,097 --> 00:14:51,432
and goes to reception to talk to you.

293
00:14:52,100 --> 00:14:53,267
We're friends.

294
00:14:53,434 --> 00:14:54,435
Apparently.

295
00:14:55,186 --> 00:14:56,521
Very nicely done.

296
00:14:56,604 --> 00:15:00,733
Okay, so, I think that's H.O.R. for Stanley and H.O. for Phyllis.

297
00:15:00,817 --> 00:15:02,485
Are you calling me a ho?

298
00:15:02,568 --> 00:15:03,569
Oh, my God.

299
00:15:04,362 --> 00:15:06,447
Phyllis coming alive, I like it.

300
00:15:06,531 --> 00:15:09,784
Question. What about car-pooling? Who pays for the gas?

301
00:15:09,867 --> 00:15:11,244
We take separate cars.

302
00:15:11,327 --> 00:15:13,347
Okay, question. Can sometimes I drive your car and you drive mine?

303
00:15:13,371 --> 00:15:15,164
Why would we do that? Just for fun.

304
00:15:15,248 --> 00:15:16,290
No. Okay, question.

305
00:15:16,374 --> 00:15:17,934
Who's the primary on the fire insurance?

306
00:15:19,085 --> 00:15:20,086
Game over.

307
00:15:21,254 --> 00:15:23,131
Offer revoked, Dwight.

308
00:15:23,714 --> 00:15:27,051
I'm sorry, but you reach out and you try to be a nice guy

309
00:15:27,135 --> 00:15:29,929
and help out a friend and this is what happens.

310
00:15:30,012 --> 00:15:31,764
This is what I get.

311
00:15:31,848 --> 00:15:35,101
Oh, God, I'm... Okay.

312
00:15:37,270 --> 00:15:38,813
<i>Thank God.</i>

313
00:15:38,896 --> 00:15:42,692
It was nice of him to offer, but I live in a nine-bedroom farmhouse.

314
00:15:43,234 --> 00:15:47,071
I have my own crossbow range. It's a perfect situation for me.

315
00:15:47,363 --> 00:15:49,240
Although, two bathrooms would have been nice.

316
00:15:49,323 --> 00:15:50,741
We just have the one.

317
00:15:52,243 --> 00:15:53,828
And it's under the porch.

318
00:15:56,456 --> 00:15:58,583
Who had somebody from Vance Refrigeration?

319
00:15:58,666 --> 00:16:00,293
I did. Ryan Howard.

320
00:16:01,002 --> 00:16:03,087
Ryan, gold medal.

321
00:16:03,963 --> 00:16:06,215
I made something for our closing ceremonies.

322
00:16:06,299 --> 00:16:10,470
What? Oh, my God. Where did you have time to make that?

323
00:16:10,636 --> 00:16:14,765
Automatic voicemail. All right, Pam, all right.

324
00:16:15,850 --> 00:16:17,226
Nice work.

325
00:16:26,694 --> 00:16:29,447
You know, you can always refinance your mortgage.

326
00:16:29,530 --> 00:16:32,283
We had a 15-year on our beet farm, we paid it off early.

327
00:16:32,366 --> 00:16:35,828
Yeah, well, you know what? Nobody cares about your stupid beet farm.

328
00:16:35,912 --> 00:16:37,413
Beets are the worst.

329
00:16:37,497 --> 00:16:39,081
People love beets. Nobody likes beets.

330
00:16:39,165 --> 00:16:40,166
Everyone loves beets.

331
00:16:40,249 --> 00:16:42,460
Nobody likes beets, Dwight.

332
00:16:42,793 --> 00:16:45,630
Why don't you grow something that everybody does like?

333
00:16:45,713 --> 00:16:47,632
You should grow candy.

334
00:16:49,008 --> 00:16:51,594
I'd love a piece of candy right now.

335
00:16:52,136 --> 00:16:53,554
Not a beet.

336
00:16:54,222 --> 00:16:56,474
Let's get this roof going. Stop it!

337
00:16:56,682 --> 00:16:58,559
Final lap, final lap.

338
00:16:58,643 --> 00:17:02,813
The time to beat is 1:15. Time to beat is one minute, 15 seconds.

339
00:17:02,897 --> 00:17:03,898
Here they come!

340
00:17:03,981 --> 00:17:06,734
Go, go, go!

341
00:17:10,154 --> 00:17:11,280
Guys.

342
00:17:13,407 --> 00:17:15,201
What is going on? Nothing.

343
00:17:15,868 --> 00:17:18,120
Guys, timer's still going.

344
00:17:18,746 --> 00:17:20,373
That's my stopwatch.

345
00:17:38,933 --> 00:17:40,768
Here you go. All done.

346
00:17:40,893 --> 00:17:41,894
Great.

347
00:17:42,270 --> 00:17:44,397
<i>Yeah, I filled out the expense reports.</i>

348
00:17:44,564 --> 00:17:48,901
That took about five minutes and then I closed two sales at lunchtime,

349
00:17:49,569 --> 00:17:52,905
so, about as productive as any other day,

350
00:17:53,573 --> 00:17:55,116
if not more so.

351
00:18:02,665 --> 00:18:04,667
<i>I figured I could throw it away now</i>

352
00:18:05,126 --> 00:18:08,796
or I could keep it for a couple of months and then throw it away.

353
00:18:09,922 --> 00:18:11,762
I mean, it was really nice of Pam to make them,

354
00:18:11,799 --> 00:18:13,342
but what am I going to do

355
00:18:13,426 --> 00:18:17,763
with a gold medal made of paperclips and an old yogurt lid?

356
00:18:27,898 --> 00:18:28,899
Hey.

357
00:18:28,983 --> 00:18:29,984
I have 59 voicemails.

358
00:18:31,861 --> 00:18:35,865
Hey, can you ignore those and do something for me, instead?

359
00:18:36,365 --> 00:18:37,825
Sure.

360
00:18:37,908 --> 00:18:40,494
Okay, today, 5:00,

361
00:18:41,537 --> 00:18:43,331
closing ceremonies.

362
00:18:43,414 --> 00:18:44,415
Really?

363
00:18:44,498 --> 00:18:45,916
Notify the athletes.

364
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:47,209
Cool.

365
00:18:50,004 --> 00:18:51,255
Michael?

366
00:18:51,339 --> 00:18:52,632
Yeah. Jim. Slim Jim.

367
00:18:53,758 --> 00:18:56,052
What's going on? Nothing.

368
00:18:56,135 --> 00:18:58,721
I just wanted to congratulate you on your condo.

369
00:18:58,846 --> 00:19:01,265
Oh, thanks, thanks. It's very cool.

370
00:19:01,432 --> 00:19:04,185
It's three bedroom, gay-friendly. Nice.

371
00:19:04,268 --> 00:19:05,353
You know.

372
00:19:05,436 --> 00:19:07,247
Hey, would you mind coming out here for a second?

373
00:19:07,271 --> 00:19:09,065
I just have something for you.

374
00:19:09,148 --> 00:19:10,149
Really?

375
00:19:12,401 --> 00:19:13,778
What's this?

376
00:19:14,070 --> 00:19:16,906
These are the closing ceremonies. Step up.

377
00:19:19,909 --> 00:19:21,202
Here, on the top one.

378
00:19:22,662 --> 00:19:26,415
Congratulations to Michael, because he closed on his condo,

379
00:19:26,749 --> 00:19:28,292
so gold medal.

380
00:19:33,964 --> 00:19:37,176
I don't really know what to say.

381
00:19:40,846 --> 00:19:43,849
I'm not one for making speeches,

382
00:19:44,934 --> 00:19:47,561
but my heart is very full at this moment.

383
00:19:50,523 --> 00:19:53,359
And for Dwight Schrute, the silver medal.

384
00:19:55,277 --> 00:19:56,904
Get on up here, Dwight.

385
00:20:02,827 --> 00:20:03,953
Silver medal.

386
00:20:04,036 --> 00:20:05,371
Yeah, not as good as gold.

387
00:20:12,670 --> 00:20:14,630
Why are you playing the National Anthem?

388
00:20:16,006 --> 00:20:18,300
'Cause your condo's in America.

389
00:20:25,433 --> 00:20:26,851
What the hell is that?

390
00:20:26,934 --> 00:20:28,436
Those are the doves.

391
00:20:57,256 --> 00:20:59,133
All right.

